d the equation of the tangent at the point $(1+\\pi ,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =0(x-(1+\\pi )) \\\\ y+3 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f18fe80-b975-5243-8971-ac37a514c459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 x^{2} - \\sin{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491412} {:answer/id #uuid "866b680e-b95e-59e9-a274-19e8c2a75d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 x^{2} - \\cos{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491416} {:answer/id #uuid "a2356a72-59b5-5c9c-b674-716424d158d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 x^{2} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491419} {:answer/id #uuid "d43c8d95-d21a-5c9b-9163-9316a2bc2a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 x^{2} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491426} {:answer/id #uuid "ef497264-895e-54be-869a-0733bc78f89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 x^{2} - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef497264-895e-54be-869a-0733bc78f89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 x^{2} - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491433} :answer-field/id #uuid "0908c732-21de-5c29-bd3c-fcc27be47e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edc669f2-8811-573d-b7ea-ebf03a0592e1" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)-6{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)$ and $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=12-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12-2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-18{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12-2x)-18{x}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "340044b2-b48d-5eb9-817c-624479f49ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "340044b2-b48d-5eb9-817c-624479f49ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491411} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8942c70-e5be-58bd-9441-c89f4912e367" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "efb4aba1-7652-55ac-8883-4dc872ffd09b" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of $y =\\sin \\left(\\pi \\left(8x^{2} + 2x - 2\\right)\\right)$ at $x = 1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (16x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (16x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (16\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8\\cdot1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =18\\pi \\cos(8\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18\\pi \\cdot (1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18 \\pi\n\\end{align}\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{18 \\pi}.\n\\]"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1117cafb-bea7-5676-bd0f-63daf7595db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 11 x + 11 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491410} {:answer/id #uuid "76c93819-5f94-5f71-9207-90527902ad17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491414} {:answer/id #uuid "aba5c3d1-b904-56ff-b6f0-a46b86905b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x - 12 - \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491423} {:answer/id #uuid "dd23d842-a5d1-5918-948e-36db4983f82a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x - 11 + \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491428} {:answer/id #uuid "fa02d377-8768-52fe-b201-deaea37828d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c93819-5f94-5f71-9207-90527902ad17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491414} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c494561-b4d8-5b70-a243-062936e00deb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f70d4be3-a4f6-5618-b5df-d3aca4e557dc" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)$ at the point $(1+\\pi ,-11).$" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=1+\\pi $ is given by ${f}^{′}(1+\\pi ).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1+\\pi ,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1+\\pi ) & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1+\\pi -1) \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(1+\\pi ,-11):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-11) & =0(x-(1+\\pi )) \\\\ y+11 & =0 \\\\ y & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72ccc92d-1a43-5830-a890-7c4034c25b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(2\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72ccc92d-1a43-5830-a890-7c4034c25b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(2\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544343} :answer-field/id #uuid "6801a841-fd4f-5ab4-9b8b-02b1c63e34e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39c6a843-fd29-52a1-8f7b-ab703e4af195" :question/math-academy-id "q-133227" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,$  with respect to $\\theta .$\n\n${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our function as the composite function\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=2\\theta .\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}u}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta } \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16850524-a400-5f1a-93bb-271f14089db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574984} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf92b52-0c3d-5dbd-ba42-bfbacd464848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575079} {:answer/id #uuid "527c209a-b5e6-511e-8fdd-5aa7990f0dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575093} {:answer/id #uuid "9111cb01-85f7-57f5-863f-e240e866660b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575200} {:answer/id #uuid "9e164b4d-5b5c-55c9-b47c-f00bb1a5bfc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e164b4d-5b5c-55c9-b47c-f00bb1a5bfc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575223} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd275e51-d3ee-5c23-a80e-981590060edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf906107-b91f-584f-aecf-00f5767c9a3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48637" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ at the point where $(\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is given by ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}]}
